<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">Br J Cancer</journal-id><journal-title>British Journal of Cancer</journal-title><issn pub-type="ppub">0007-0920</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1532-1827</issn></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">465296</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2009883</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Research Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>Effect of carrageenan on the non-specific resistance of mice to injected syngeneic tumour cells, alone or in mixtures.</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Wu</surname><given-names>R. L.</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Kearney</surname><given-names>R.</given-names></name></contrib></contrib-group><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><month>3</month><year>1979</year></pub-date><volume>39</volume><issue>3</issue><fpage>241</fpage><lpage>246</lpage><abstract><p>The mechanisms of non-specific resistance to syngeneic methylcholanthrene-induced fibrosarcomas of mice were investigated. Results showed that a small tumour graft of 0.05 X 10(5) cells is greatly enhanced in growth when admixed with large numbers of cell fragments, killed cells or viable non-replicating cells. The enhancement of small tumour grafts in cell mixtures was found to be non-specific. Carrageenan, a known anti-macrophage agent, significantly increased tumour growth in normal mice. However, it did not enhance the increased tumour growth of 0.05 X 10(5) cells mixed with 10(6) viable, non-replicating mitomycin C-treated tumour cells. The latter observation indicates that carrageenan and admixed cells interfere with the same tumour-inhibitory mechanism and therefore cannot produce additive effects. The results give support to the concept of a non-specific macrophage "surveillance" system which appears crucial in controlling tumour growth, since it determines the establishment of small numbers of tumour cells while they can still be easily destroyed.</p></abstract></article-meta></front><body><supplementary-material content-type="scanned-pages"><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00149-0027.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="241" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00149-0028.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="242" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00149-0029.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="243" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00149-0030.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="244" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00149-0031.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="245" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00149-0032.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="246" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/></supplementary-material></body></article>